<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ide Anak Agung Gde Agung</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1921">1921</a>–<a href="/years/1999">1999</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ide Anak Agung Gde Agung was a prince of the Gianyar royal house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> who became an Indonesian statesman and historian. He was prime minister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-east-indonesia">State of East Indonesia</a> in the federal period and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>'s foreign minister from 1955 to 1956. He was named a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-hero-of-indonesia">National Hero of Indonesia</a> in 2007.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ide-anak-agung-gde-agung.jpg" alt="Ide Anak Agung Gde Agung" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NIGIS [Netherlands Indies Government Information Service] · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anak_Agoeng_Gde_Agoeng,_Minister_of_the_Interior.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ide Anak Agung Gde Agung was born in Gianyar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>, on 21 July 1921, the son of Raja Anak Gunung Ngurah Agung, the Raja of Gianyar. Interested in history from childhood, he went to the Hollandsche-Inlandsche School, then to MULO and the Algemeene Middelbare School, studied law at the Rechtshogeschool in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> and later took a doctorate in history at the University of Utrecht. On 23 August 1943, during the Japanese occupation and after his father had been detained on suspicion of treason, he became Raja of Gianyar at the age of 22.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His political career began in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-east-indonesia">State of East Indonesia</a>, where he was prime minister from December 1947 to December 1949 under Tjokorda Gde Raka Soekawati. In 1948 he founded and led the Federal Consultative Assembly, the association of federal states and territories formed to strengthen their hand in the negotiations between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, and he played a decisive part in the Round Table Conference that led to Dutch recognition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesian-independence">Indonesian independence</a>. After the transfer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> on 27 December 1949 he was minister of the interior in the short-lived Republic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> of Indonesia.</p><p>His federalist sympathies set him against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a>, who wanted a unitary republic. After the federal system was abolished in 1950 he served as ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and as foreign minister he took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-new-guinea">West New Guinea</a> dispute. He was imprisoned from 1962 to 1966 without trial, until the foreign minister of the New Order, Adam Malik, released him and restored him to a senior post; he was again ambassador to Austria.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Abroad he wrote Twenty Years Indonesian foreign policy 1945–1965, a 660-page history arguing that Indonesian foreign policy rested on independence and action, free of foreign powers, and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> abandoned it by aligning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and confronting Malaysia. He died in Gianyar on 22 April 1999. Besides the title of National Hero, he received the Bintang Mahaputra Adipradana, and a street in the Mega Kuningan district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a> bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ide Anak Agung Gde Agung” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under colonial rule Ubud became the centre of a famous artistic revival, and Gianyar royals, including <b>Ide Anak Agung Gde Agung</b>, became Indonesian statesmen.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-gianyar">Kingdom of Gianyar</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Gianyar, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. Died in Gianyar, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-8.47,115.28&amp;year=1971" aria-label="Gianyar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ide Anak Agung Gde Agung"><image href="/maps/globes/g12000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4601 5721h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4601 5721h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-8.47,115.28&amp;year=1971">What was here in 1971? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
